{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-cap-exhaustion-recovery", "file": "references/cap-exhaustion-session-lesson.md", "content": "# Cap-exhaustion recovery lesson

In a streamed ARC instance, one pre-existing task program was immediately executed as required and rejected. Across eight demo requests, the examples varied substantially in dimensions, palette, edge layouts, and marker configurations. Several plausible geometric families appeared (edge-to-edge lines, marker rays, and gap filling), but no single transform was encoded and shown to reproduce all exact pairs. After rejection, the task program was repeatedly overwritten with speculative candidates and eventually an identity-like function; multiple rejected submissions then repeated either the same output or a cosmetic variation. Reaching the demo cap did not resolve the underlying rule.

Reusable practice: preserve a candidate separately until full-grid regressions pass, retain exact demo fixtures independently, and after each wrong verdict discard the full tested rule. Never recycle a rejected output as if transform execution made it new. In particular, validate the generated test grid by running the final candidate after the last code edit; do not rely on a prior terminal output that belonged to different code. The episode did not establish the puzzle's hidden rule; this note records the observed failure-control lesson only.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HPL-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-3/play/users/u078/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzles/continual-arc-cap-exhaustion-recovery/references/cap-exhaustion-session-lesson.md"}

